WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
Super Spike V-Ball, Bump, Set, Spike, Kaboom!

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:17.060
Super Spike V-Ball, Bump, Set, Spike, Kaboom!

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:29.740
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.680
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.080
And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:39.820
Okay, so last week we played a pinball game and that was weird because like we just played a pinball game right before that.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.960
Now volleyball is having a moment.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.000
Yeah, and we kind of just played a volleyball game before that.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.220
Absolutely true.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.220
And so we have to come up with, you know, two hours of content here about volleyball.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.500
Does anybody...

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.380
About like 30 minutes.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.480
Can we do 30 minutes?

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.360
I can do 30. Yeah, well, two times .25 speed.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.580
Okay, yeah, let's do that.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.560
I think what helps this version of volleyball is that we don't need to know any athletes.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.780
In the last episode, we were really confused about who those guys were or if they were even famous or if people would have known about them.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:22.440
In this game, you just have to know Billy and Jimmy, who were the heroes of the Double Dragon series.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.820
They show up in this game, which is really freaking cool.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.740
Oh, you know, I didn't even notice that, but...

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.240
It's like Easter egg stuff.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.140
It's not like you were...

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.780
You're not supposed to be like, oh, they have their Double Dragon powers.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.160
Like, no, they're just the same names.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.500
And I guess like, I don't really remember what they look like in the arcade or whatever, but I guess you...

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.320
If you played those games enough, you would make the connection that it's more than just the names that they have.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.960
The likeliness. The likeness.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.340
The likeliness. We're using my words.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.880
I got it. It's very likely that they are Billy and Jimmy.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.960
Yeah, yeah. You can't say with certainty that anything is anything.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.960
Nothing is nothing, though.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.380
We all can agree on that.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.860
But volleyball.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:07.760
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.780
Well, so starting with just the fact that we've played volleyball games before, don't talk about this game.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.200
What are you looking for in a volleyball game?

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.040
What's going to like tease us a little bit?

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.460
What would it take for a volleyball game to be on the essential games list?

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.440
I think it would have to be something along the lines of what...

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:34.420
I've talked about my brief tryst with Mario Tennis before on this podcast.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:48.840
And I think it would have to be something that adds enough flavor to such a simple game to, you know, to hold my attention for hours on end.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:54.620
And that's something that Mario Tennis 64 or whatever did for tennis.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.600
And I haven't really come across that for volleyball yet.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:05.040
But I guess it's just like an expressiveness to the game that I would want.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.000
Yeah, it's hard for me to say...

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.060
Like, I do think that it's possible for a volleyball game to do something that makes me want to put on the essential games list.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.140
I don't know what that is, though, is the problem.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.440
Like, I feel like I'd know it when I'd see it.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.940
And if that doesn't spoil my essential games list vote for you, I don't know what will.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.820
Right. What about you, Mike?

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.680
Well, you know, it's a funny question because it's mine and I only ask funny questions.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.200
I agree with Joe that, like, I don't think I was going...

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.820
I feel like I'm a little...

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.480
I'm not great at this show because I kind of hear that it's a volleyball game and my mind might as well be made up beforehand.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.700
And that's not the objective of this show.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.320
We're supposed to come in with no opinions.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.100
But I've played some volleyball games.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.440
I've played some risque volleyball games.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.380
Uh-huh. In my video game career.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.360
And here we are with volleyball on the NES.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.520
And I'm kind of like, well, I've played volleyball on more advanced systems.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:16.100
And I don't know, you know, like, I hate to use that as a way to say, like, no, it's not gonna be essential because there's other iterations.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:26.740
But, like, what could the basic version of volleyball do here to set itself apart from deeper, more interesting 3D versions of volleyball?

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.780
And so I guess that's what we're gonna have to get into now.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:36.640
But before we talk about any kind of gameplay, I want to talk about this character select screen.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:44.640
Okay. Because they give you, like, you know, you choose just one character and then they, like, bring a teammate along.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.800
Yeah, them and their clone.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:05:01.100
I think they should have leaned into it and actually, like, had this take place in the future where, like, even Chicago was sort of like in a tropical climate where, you know, everyone can always year-round play beach volleyball shirtless.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:11.440
And there's cloning and every top athlete has a clone as insurance for their career.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.080
And obviously they're also genetically modified to keep them in top.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.480
That's terrible insurance, though, if they're both playing in the games at the same time and they could both get injured.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:27.800
I mean, I think the chances of them both being injured are lower than one person being injured.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:35.660
But also, if it's me and my clone are the team and my clone is my insurance, if I get injured, they still gotta replace me because my clone is my teammate.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.100
You can't replace me and still be the teammate.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.620
All right, you've got other clones in the bank.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:49.620
Oh, okay. You know, like, you've got probably a fridge somewhere that's got some at, like, various points in their gestation, you know?

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:50.880
Like, that's how it would work.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:54.520
I gotcha. I think that even maybe the actual athlete is never even playing.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.400
It's all his clones.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:56.500
He's never even played volleyball.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.320
I mean, that's the question, you know?

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:04.640
But this system where the character you choose just brings along a friend, right?

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:05.940
Imagine a story...

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:06.760
Yeah, clone, sure.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.640
I mean, I get why you're saying clone because it is the same exact sprite on screen.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:18.260
But, like, what I'm saying here is imagine if it was a story-based game, not a sports game, right?

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:28.060
Where, like, you're choosing your main character in a JRPG quest, but they happen to also, like, bring along the only other member of your party.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:31.180
But you don't get to know any details about them or anything.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.120
They're just, like, they're in for the ride.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.640
And it turns out, like, the character you picked has all these great stats, but maybe their teammate doesn't.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.280
That's not the case here because, as Sean mentioned, it's just two clones, so it's the same person twice.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.880
But, like, the implication is bad.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.680
I think the implication's wrong.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:55.840
I guess also to what you're saying, it's a missed opportunity to give you, the player, the option to be like,

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:01.820
okay, I want to pick so-and-so because he's good at spiking, but then I want the other teammate to be good at defense.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.500
You know, it's like, no, you can have two players that are good at defense, or you can have two players that are good at, you know, attacking.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:17.380
Well, there's also too small of a roster for, I think, those decisions to really make much of a difference.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:24.360
And I also, I played as a few different variations, and I couldn't really, like, there might be a difference,

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:28.480
but I couldn't find, like, it didn't feel crazy different.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.340
It's still the same experience.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.880
Yeah. I mean, now that you mention it, I'm not sure what difference there was in any of these,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.960
other than, like, some of the AI that you're playing against was just harder,

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:43.700
but I didn't think, like, oh, these guys, wow, these guys are, their defense is great.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.080
I never thought like that.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:50.300
On the character select screen for Al, one of the characters you can choose to be,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:59.800
he has just a different color text from the rest of the screen on his letter U in the word U.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.060
Is that symbolic of anything?

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:03.500
Should we be reading into that?

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:09.440
I like this.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.300
Have we ever done any, have we ever talked about something like this before?

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.940
It's not like, oh, the rest of it's red and this is purple, or, you know, something like that.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.700
Just. No, this is.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.480
It's subtle. This is the developer talking to us.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.340
This is a sign. It's a cry for help.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:36.280
Also, in the character select screen, you have the unusual ability to select your state as well.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:43.560
The stages in the game are Daytona Beach, New York City, Chicago, Las Vegas, and Los Angeles,

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.340
and then eventually there's the World Cup as well.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:52.580
But the state you select, as far as I know, doesn't matter at all?

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.960
No, I think it's just for customization purposes.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.080
But is it ever mentioned in the game?

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.780
Like, oh, and here they are from New York, the boys.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:06.960
I think when you win, it tells you, like, oh, it was this team from Alaska.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:13.720
Yeah, like when you win the final thing, it says, like, yeah, great job, New York, or whatever.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.940
Right. I guess at that point, just, like, let me choose my last name.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:24.360
Yeah, but I mean, I guess if you're doing, like, the, you're doing, like, an American tour, or whatever you call it,

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:30.880
like, it's cool to be like, oh, I'm picking my state, or I'm just picking a state to be from, like, you are,

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:37.160
there's other, there's American cities that you're going to, so it's like, oh, okay, I get to pick the American state that I'm from.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:44.320
Yeah, but assume that you picked the state that you're from, and you're the one doing all the controlling, and you win the tournament,

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:50.900
and then it presents it like it's two other guys that you are not that won the tournament, and they're also from New York.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.240
It's like, oh, there's two guys better than me in my state, but I'm the one who did all the work.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.800
Wait, that's how I feel watching the Olympics.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.160
But you're the one doing all the work at the Olympics, we know.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.240
Yes. Yeah, you're the one watching.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.820
My tax dollars paid for their vacation to Sochi.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:13.640
But when you say two other people, are you talking about the two characters that you were playing as?

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:15.300
Yeah, yeah, because, like, you're not them.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:21.000
That's what I'm saying, like, take your last name instead as the character selection thing, not, like, the same.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.100
So they're at least, like, your distant cousins.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:24.760
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.200
Now it's like they're your rivals.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:27.820
Your two identical clone twin cousins.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.540
Okay. Yeah, it was a stretch, right?

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:31.840
I agree. I got nothing.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:11:04.460
Yeah. So we're talking about the gameplay now because I decided that it's time for that section.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.380
Okay. And there's a lot of controls here, actually.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:14.480
I thought the game was kind of simple at first before I looked at the manual, and I was just like, all right, cool.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:22.440
You can jump and spike, or you can just volley the ball back to the other you and do it that way.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:32.160
But, like, no, there's a lot of options here, and I guess, like, they will come into play the longer, you know, like, as the computers get smarter and your reflexes need to be faster.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:36.840
But when you start this game, you don't need to know any of that.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:40.660
No. No, it's very simple.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.420
It's easy to get a hang of.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.600
One is, like, you know, interact with ball, and one is jump.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:12:00.980
And the different, like, directional inputs that you can use while using those buttons kind of gives you all the effects that you need.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:09.420
And timing with jumps and spikes has a pretty insane effect on things.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.680
I was actually surprised early when I was playing the game.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:17.360
I was like, okay, bump, set, and spike.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.200
And then bump, set, and spike.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:23.520
And that was just sort of like, okay, I'm kind of getting bored at this.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:31.340
But then I realized that, like, you don't really need to wait for the ball to get to a certain point spiking.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:35.080
Like, you could have it, like, bump, set, spike.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:41.700
And you could kind of get a big kaboom effect, and the player flies back.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:49.300
And it is a little bit of that, like, expressiveness that I was talking about in what I'm looking for in these simpler sports games.

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.680
I don't know how you guys felt about that.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:59.480
Yeah, but it does still need, you do still need to, like, it is unforgiving in the way that, like, you're lined up.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:03.820
It doesn't, like, give you any help of, like, oh, you almost hit the ball, so we're going to hit the ball.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:04.860
Like, I noticed that right away.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.280
I noticed what you're talking about, too, where you can, like, time it in different ways.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.480
But you can easily whiff on the ball.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:12.740
Oh, absolutely.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:19.180
And I think that that is, I don't remember if we kind of had a control scheme like this in other volleyball games.

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:23.700
But it's just because there's a separate button for jump and then interact with the ball.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:29.960
So you have to jump, and then when you're lined up perfectly to where you want to be, where the ball is close enough to you, whatever,

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:31.940
then you hit the button to spike.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:37.960
And I feel like because of that, that made it feel more satisfying to actually do it.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:45.300
And also, like, I'm in, I'm in, like, it takes more skill than, like, there just being a spike button.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:50.020
Or I feel like in one of them we had, like, A and B is the spike button or something, like, together.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:55.440
Whereas, like, this is, like, no, I'm actually, it feels a little more related to volleyball

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:01.280
because I'm actually having to time it like I would have to if I was using my actual arm on an actual ball.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:07.560
Yeah, it feels like there's a bit of a higher skill ceiling than what I would have expected with the simplicity of the control.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:15.080
Right. And what Sean was saying with the kaboom, like, there's a whole, like, flair there to this game with the wop and kaboom and stuff

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:21.660
that, like, actually appears on screen as you're hitting that makes it a little, you know, fun and arcade-y.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.240
But at the same time, you know, with the skill ceiling.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:31.340
But this game also has the ability to make you feel really cool and then immediately really uncool

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.500
when you do, like, the kaboom, right?

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.000
And it's like, wow, look at that.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.720
Like, this is fast action.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.260
Like, that's me. I'm doing that stuff.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:44.160
And then to Joe's point, like, when you whiff, like, when you just don't time correctly

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:52.340
and you swing too early and then the ball just falls into the sand, it's like, it feels really bad

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:59.040
and it's, you know, there's no more, like, there's no more visual flair for that kind of thing that happens.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:03.540
It just feels like the whole world paused and watched you fail when you whiff.

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.900
I think that's supposed to be that way.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.440
Absolutely. I would want that.

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.520
Well, I don't know if I would want that, but I think that that's effective game design that, like,

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.680
one minute you're having fun and then the next minute you're in timeout.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:23.940
Well, even when you get hit with the ball, like, if someone uses that kaboom move and it hits you,

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:29.200
like, there's, yeah, there's a knockdown, like, a little bit of time where you got to get up

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.220
and get to the ball while your partner's setting it.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:33.460
Like, you know, that adds a little bit to it as well.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:41.200
But it feels so easy early on that I felt like, provided I was just timing my jumps and my spikes

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:46.940
correctly, the rest of the game was this weird autoplay where as long as I was spamming the A button

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:52.660
before the opponent even returned the ball over to me, because your players don't do anything when the ball isn't, like,

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:59.100
in their vicinity, it seemed like the players just automatically targeted and volleyed the ball,

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:06.600
which is, like, bad practice for what eventually happens in the game where you need to be more reflexive and fast-paced.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:09.660
Sometimes you almost got to be, like, preemptive.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:11.480
Right, right. At least maybe me.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.640
Like, I felt like I had to try and figure out, okay, where are they going to hit this now and kind of get in position.

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:17.380
Yeah, you got to worry about spacing.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:28.460
The good thing is, and it's definitely a frustration saver, is it doesn't matter which of your clones they spike it at.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:38.060
If you hit the interact button, they will, like, set it or attempt to, at least, if you're nearby.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:50.920
But you still need to worry about your spacing so that there isn't just a wide-open part of the sand that is just open for them to spike it at.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:57.620
Yeah. One thing, though, that did throw me off kind of about that at first, and granted I got used to it,

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:03.980
is that, like, if the ball is going towards the player that you're not playing as at the moment,

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:08.680
sometimes, or maybe all the time, you just switch to controlling that person.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.220
Yeah, that's sort of what I meant.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:17.820
Yeah, that actually kind of bugged me at first, because I would be actively controlling the other guy,

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:23.920
trying to get him to a position just because, like, just out of instinct, because that's the person I'm controlling,

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:29.820
and then suddenly in the middle of me controlling him, now I'm controlling the first guy because he's closer to the ball or something,

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:34.620
but I'm already pushing the D-pad in one direction, so he just goes the wrong way or something.

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:36.300
I did get used to it after a while.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.640
It became, like, kind of just intuitive of, like, who I was going to be controlling,

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.600
but at first that was really a little jarring.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.600
